WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.317 --> 00:00:19.099
I stood in front of Baker Street, trying to contain my excitement.

2
00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:21.020
Next to me was Mr.

3
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.160
Jones, the inspector from Scotland Yard.

4
00:00:23.921 --> 00:00:27.762
Mr. Merryweather of the City and Suburban Bank was also standing nearby.

5
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:31.044
And of course, there was my friend Sherlock Holmes.

6
00:00:31.892 --> 00:00:36.517
"We are hunting in couples again, Doctor," said the inspector, sensing my mood.

7
00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:43.344
"Your friend is wonderful at starting the chase, but he always calls this old dog to help him

8
00:00:43.444 --> 00:00:45.486
catch his fox."

9
00:00:45.506 --> 00:00:49.350
"I hope it doesn't end in a wild goose chase," added Mr.

10
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:50.752
Merryweather pessimistically.

11
00:00:50.772 --> 00:00:53.094
"You can be confident in Mr.

12
00:00:53.154 --> 00:00:54.696
Holmes," said the inspector.

13
00:00:55.612 --> 00:01:00.797
"Although he has his own methods, and they tend to be theoretical and a bit unconventional, he is

14
00:01:00.857 --> 00:01:02.158
a first-rate detective.

15
00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:05.901
He has facilitated our work on a couple of cases."

16
00:01:05.921 --> 00:01:08.223
"If you say so, Mr.

17
00:01:08.263 --> 00:01:10.225
Jones," said the vice president.

18
00:01:11.045 --> 00:01:14.948
"I'm just a little disappointed about missing my weekly card game.

19
00:01:15.769 --> 00:01:20.633
It's the first Saturday night in 27 years that I have missed."

20
00:01:20.653 --> 00:01:28.002
"I think you will find," said Holmes, "that you will play for much higher stakes tonight than ever

21
00:01:28.062 --> 00:01:33.285
before, and that you will find this game more exciting than any hand of cards.

22
00:01:34.046 --> 00:01:35.426
Your stakes tonight, Mr.

23
00:01:35.446 --> 00:01:41.930
Merryweather, are a treasure of gold; and for you, Jones, it will be a chance to apprehend the

24
00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.311
top man on your most-wanted list."

25
00:01:44.331 --> 00:01:48.113
"John Clay murderer, thief, and master forger?

26
00:01:48.954 --> 00:01:54.473
I can think of no other criminal in London that I would rather catch," stated the inspector wishfully.

27
00:01:54.493 --> 00:01:55.874
"John Clay?"

28
00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:56.955
I asked.

29
00:01:57.776 --> 00:02:00.998
The name sounded familiar, but I couldn't remember the details.

30
00:02:01.018 --> 00:02:04.480
"Yes, my dear doctor," said the inspector.

31
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.642
"He is a remarkable man.

32
00:02:06.922 --> 00:02:13.707
His grandfather was a royal duke, and John himself has attended the finest schools, including Oxford.

33
00:02:14.407 --> 00:02:15.768
He is quite shrewd.

34
00:02:16.208 --> 00:02:20.451
He can rob a bank in Scotland one week and help to build an orphanage in Cornwall the

35
00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:26.937
next. We have followed his trail for years, but he always manages to stay just a couple of

36
00:02:26.997 --> 00:02:27.857
steps ahead of us."

37
00:02:27.877 --> 00:02:30.640
"I hope to introduce you tonight.

38
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.102
I believe him to be none other than Vincent Spaulding, Mr.

39
00:02:34.142 --> 00:02:37.685
Wilson's assistant," said Holmes, checking his watch.

40
00:02:37.705 --> 00:02:40.007
"It is after ten; we should go."

41
00:02:40.688 --> 00:02:41.789
Mr. Jones and Mr.

42
00:02:41.809 --> 00:02:45.692
Merryweather left in the first carriage, and Holmes and I followed in the second.

43
00:02:46.793 --> 00:02:50.336
Holmes didn't say a word during the ride, and I respected his silence.

44
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.404
I knew that our lives might very well depend on the accuracy of his thoughts.

45
00:02:56.365 --> 00:03:00.108
The night was foggy, and the gas-lit streets were empty and quiet.

46
00:03:01.008 --> 00:03:05.272
Out the window of the carriage, I recognized the same street we had walked down earlier in the

47
00:03:05.312 --> 00:03:09.836
day. We stopped in front of the Coburg branch of the City and Suburban Bank.

48
00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:16.121
Mr. Merryweather took us into the building, down a flight of stairs to the cellar, through several locked

49
00:03:16.221 --> 00:03:18.924
iron gates, and down many dark passages.

50
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.641
"You are not vulnerable from above," observed Holmes after we passed through another iron gate.

51
00:03:25.462 --> 00:03:27.342
"Nor from below," said Mr.

52
00:03:27.362 --> 00:03:30.543
Merryweather confidently, stomping on the floor with the heel of his shoe.

53
00:03:30.563 --> 00:03:33.504
"Oh!" he exclaimed in surprise.

54
00:03:33.524 --> 00:03:35.685
"It sounds quite hollow."

55
00:03:35.705 --> 00:03:39.326
"Please be quiet," said Holmes, irritated.

56
00:03:39.346 --> 00:03:42.987
"You have just risked the success of this adventure.

57
00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:46.548
Now stand over there and stay out of our way."

58
00:03:47.444 --> 00:03:52.946
Mr. Merryweather looked injured but did as he was told, while Holmes took the lantern.

59
00:03:53.727 --> 00:03:56.708
With a magnifying glass, the detective examined the floor.

60
00:03:57.528 --> 00:04:00.830
When he was finished, he said, "We have about an hour.

61
00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.591
They won't attempt anything until they are sure Mr.

62
00:04:04.651 --> 00:04:05.932
Wilson is in bed.

63
00:04:06.732 --> 00:04:12.094
But once he is asleep, they won't lose a minute because the sooner they do their work, the

64
00:04:12.134 --> 00:04:14.615
more time they will have to make their escape."

65
00:04:15.316 --> 00:04:16.116
"What will they steal?"

66
00:04:16.772 --> 00:04:20.215
I asked, looking around at the unmarked crates and boxes.

67
00:04:20.235 --> 00:04:25.198
"Gold," said the vice president, pointing to fifteen crates in the corner.

68
00:04:25.959 --> 00:04:28.961
"Each crate contains two thousand French napoleons.

69
00:04:29.722 --> 00:04:36.367
The business transaction they were originally acquired for fell through, and we have had no opportunity to transfer

70
00:04:36.407 --> 00:04:37.948
the money to a safer location."

71
00:04:38.749 --> 00:04:42.371
Holmes took another look at his watch and said, "It is time, Mr.

72
00:04:43.564 --> 00:04:48.986
Merryweather. Once we conceal ourselves behind the boxes and crates, put a screen over the lantern."

73
00:04:49.006 --> 00:04:53.107
"These are resolute and daring men," continued Holmes.

74
00:04:53.127 --> 00:04:57.088
"Although we will surprise them, they may still do us harm.

75
00:04:57.948 --> 00:05:02.230
I urge everyone to be cautious and to make no unnecessary moves.

76
00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.911
They have but one retreat, so I hope you have done what I asked, Jones."

77
00:05:06.931 --> 00:05:10.292
"Yes," replied the inspector quietly.

78
00:05:10.816 --> 00:05:13.238
"There are two officers waiting outside Mr.

79
00:05:13.298 --> 00:05:14.059
Wilson's house."

80
00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.141
"Excellent," said Holmes.

81
00:05:16.161 --> 00:05:18.022
"All the holes are stopped."

82
00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:19.243
Holmes, Mr.

83
00:05:19.263 --> 00:05:22.826
Jones, and I took our positions behind various crates and boxes.

84
00:05:23.587 --> 00:05:28.671
We waited in the dark and perceived nothing happening until we saw light coming from under the floor.

85
00:05:29.392 --> 00:05:34.536
At first the light shone through a single crack in the concrete, but over time the floor began

86
00:05:34.576 --> 00:05:35.217
to fracture.

87
00:05:36.018 --> 00:05:38.500
It began to resemble a spider's web.

88
00:05:39.152 --> 00:05:43.356
A hole suddenly appeared in the center of the web, and a hand thrust through.

89
00:05:44.316 --> 00:05:49.421
After feeling around, the hand withdrew under the floor, and the hole began to widen.

90
00:05:50.221 --> 00:05:54.805
A clean-shaven man with a boyish face began to squeeze through the larger opening.

91
00:05:54.825 --> 00:05:59.088
"It's all clear," said the man, oblivious to our presence.

92
00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:02.872
Soon another man, a redhead, also came up from below.

93
00:06:02.892 --> 00:06:05.994
"Got you," said Holmes when Mr.

94
00:06:06.034 --> 00:06:07.976
Merryweather opened the shutter on the lantern.

95
00:06:08.576 --> 00:06:12.017
There was a moment of blindness as my eyes adjusted to the sudden light.

96
00:06:12.597 --> 00:06:17.859
Holmes had seized Clay by the collar, and Jones had grabbed the red-headed man by the shirt and

97
00:06:17.959 --> 00:06:20.319
said with a snicker, "Hello, there, ‘Mr.

98
00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:25.641
Ross.'" Unfortunately the shirt ripped, and the man dived back down the hole.

99
00:06:26.281 --> 00:06:31.902
Clay pulled a revolver out of his pocket, but before he could point it at anyone, Holmes used

100
00:06:31.942 --> 00:06:33.903
his walking stick to knock it to the floor.

101
00:06:33.923 --> 00:06:37.964
"It's no use, Clay," said Holmes to the trapped man.

102
00:06:39.004 --> 00:06:42.327
"As for your friend, there are men waiting for him at the door."

103
00:06:42.347 --> 00:06:45.329
"I must compliment you," said Clay.

104
00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:48.732
"You seem to have prepared for every possibility."

105
00:06:48.752 --> 00:06:52.135
"And I must compliment you," said Holmes.

106
00:06:52.975 --> 00:06:57.419
"The idea of the Red-Headed League was quite original and ingenious."

107
00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:05.021
While Inspector Jones handcuffed Clay and took him away, Holmes turned to the banker and said, "My expenses

108
00:07:05.101 --> 00:07:10.165
on this case have been slight, but I expect that the bank will happily reimburse them."

109
00:07:10.185 --> 00:07:14.688
"Certainly, and a reward, if you like," said Mr.

110
00:07:14.708 --> 00:07:18.631
Merryweather. "A reward won't be necessary," said Holmes.

111
00:07:18.651 --> 00:07:22.394
"The experience was gratifying enough."

112
00:07:23.214 --> 00:07:26.977
On the way back to Baker Street, I asked Holmes, "How did you know?"

113
00:07:26.997 --> 00:07:30.300
"It was perfectly obvious," he explained.

114
00:07:31.192 --> 00:07:34.235
"The purpose of that advertisement was to get Mr.

115
00:07:34.275 --> 00:07:38.118
Wilson out of his house for a certain number of hours each day.

116
00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:46.726
When I saw the assistant's pants, I remembered his fondness for photography and disappearing into the cellar, and

117
00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:49.729
I began to suspect that he was tunneling.

118
00:07:50.429 --> 00:07:53.592
When I saw the bank, I knew where he was tunneling to.

119
00:07:54.653 --> 00:07:57.556
It was all quite elementary, my dear Watson."
